1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,620
Here is something that sounds impossible.

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:04,320
A store can give you a product for free,

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,720
and still make more money than if it sold it at half price.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:09,340
Same product. Same shelf.

5
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,280
You pay five bucks an item either way.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,640
But one of these signs doubles the store's profit.

7
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,600
And, surprise, it is the "free" one.

8
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,940
Yeah. Stores would rather hand you a freebie than knock fifty percent off.

9
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:21,440
That is not generosity. That is a magic trick.

10
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,200
And you are the rabbit.

11
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,420
Stand behind the counter for a second. Look at the store's side.

12
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:28,380
A product sells for ten dollars.

13
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,673
It costs the store four dollars to make.

14
00:00:30,673 --> 00:00:32,420
Fifty percent off. You pay five.

15
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,080
The store spent four to make it.

16
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:37,080
Five minus four — the store keeps one dollar.

17
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,680
Buy one, get one free. You pay ten and take two.

18
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,960
Those two cost the store eight to make.

19
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:45,620
Ten minus eight — the store keeps two dollars. Double.

20
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,200
So it is the same five dollars an item for you.

21
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,020
But double the profit for the store.

22
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,640
And the sign never changed — it still says ten.

23
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,260
You paid full price and felt clever doing it.

24
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:56,600
Oh, and this trick is old.

25
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,080
A pottery guy named Wedgwood ran it back in the seventeen hundreds.

26
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,813
So no — you are not the first sucker. You are just the latest.

27
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:06,787
Here is the part the sign hides: what you actually spend.

28
00:01:06,787 --> 00:01:10,580
Fifty percent off, you wanted one thing, you paid five, done.

29
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:11,480
Buy one, get one free?

30
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,060
To get the "free" one, you first coughed up the full ten.

31
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:16,000
You spent twice as much. In one trip.

32
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,680
And you carried home a second one you did not need.

33
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:20,180
That is the whole game.

34
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:22,240
Fifty percent off is a smaller price.

35
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,540
Buy one, get one free is a bigger basket.

36
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:25,940
One saves your money.

37
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:28,940
The other spends it, and smiles at you while it robs you.

38
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,880
If you only wanted one, half price was the better deal.

39
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:36,000
You walked right past it, because the other sign said the magic word.

40
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,460
That magic word is "free."

41
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,980
And "free" makes your brain go a little stupid.

42
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:42,140
When something is free, you stop doing math.

43
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:45,360
You forget the boring question: do I even want a second one?

44
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,900
A scientist named Dan Ariely said it best.

45
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,300
When something is free, we forget the downside.

46
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,013
Free feels like a gift, so we grab it and switch our brains off.

47
00:01:54,013 --> 00:01:57,000
"Fifty percent off" is a number. Your brain checks numbers.

48
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,220
"Free" is a feeling. Your brain just yells "yes" and grabs.

49
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:04,020
So buy one, get one free is not selling you a discount.

50
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:08,420
It is selling you a word, and quietly stapling a full-price purchase to it.

51
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,400
You did not get a free shampoo.

52
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,280
You got a full-price shampoo, with a hostage.

53
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,280
Third trick, and it is the sneaky one.

54
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:17,873
The price on the sign never drops.

55
00:02:17,873 --> 00:02:21,380
Fifty percent off whispers: this thing is only really worth five bucks.

56
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,660
And now five is the normal price in your head.

57
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:25,950
The product looks cheap, forever.

58
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:29,640
Buy one, get one free keeps that proud ten-dollar price right there.

59
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,740
The "real" price stays high. You just got a bonus on top.

60
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:33,960
Stores love that.

61
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,880
They get the sale without admitting the thing is cheap.

62
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:38,800
And they pick this shape on purpose.

63
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,480
You want the first one a lot, and the second one, eh, not really.

64
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,540
At half price you would buy just one.

65
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:46,320
So they do not drop the price.

66
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:49,620
They shove a second one at you and charge full for the first.

67
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,300
Okay, fair is fair.

68
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,060
Buy one, get one free is not always a win for the store either.

69
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:56,280
Sometimes they lose too. Good.

70
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,640
On cheap stuff, milk, bread, eggs, there is barely any profit.

71
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,160
Give one away, and the store can actually lose money.

72
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:03,580
So why do it? Bait.

73
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:05,420
It is called a loss leader.

74
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:07,300
The free deal drags you through the door,

75
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,740
betting you will fill the rest of the cart with things that do make money.

76
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:12,380
Spoiler: you will.

77
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:14,450
And there is a cost on your side too.

78
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:19,860
Two-for-one on food you cannot finish just turns into science experiments in the back of your fridge.

79
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:24,800
Nothing screams "I saved money" like binning a free yogurt you forgot you owned.

80
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:28,140
So why does buy one, get one free beat fifty percent off?

81
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:31,280
Because the two signs are playing different games.

82
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:32,910
Fifty percent off cuts the price.

83
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:35,533
Buy one, get one free cuts your judgment.

84
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:37,000
One asks your brain a question.

85
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,520
The other hands it a gift and says, shh, relax.

86
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:41,680
This does not mean never take the deal.

87
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:43,300
Sometimes you genuinely want two.

88
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,600
Sometimes free really is free money.

89
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:50,040
Just see the second sign coming before "free" switches off your inner accountant.

90
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:51,240
Oh, one last thing.

91
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:54,020
Next time you see "buy one, get one fifty percent off"?

92
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,300
That is not free anything.

93
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:58,280
That is twenty-five percent off, in a costume.

94
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:01,380
Same shelf. Same product. Completely different game.

95
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:03,464
Now go be slightly harder to trick.

